[FINAL-TEXT] === DIAGNOSIS ===
is_anomaly: true
faulty_devices: dswa2, dswb2
diagnosis_text:
Affected component: the DHCP relay agent (isc-dhcp-relay) on the distribution switches. Mechanism: DHCPDISCOVER frames are being received on VLAN SVIs that reside on dswa2 and dswb2, but those switches are not running a relay, so the broadcasts are never forwarded to the DHCP server, causing new clients to time out. Existing clients keep working because they already hold leases and route normally.

Localization and evidence:
- dswa2 and dswb2: isc-dhcp-relay is not running and not listening on UDP 67. ps shows no dhcrelay, and ss shows no UDP :67 socket. Their relay config files are empty: /etc/default/isc-dhcp-relay has SERVERS="" and INTERFACES="" and systemctl reports the service inactive (dead).
- These same switches own the gateway VIPs for affected VLANs: dswa2 has 10.2.0.1/32 on br0.20 and 10.6.0.1/32 on br0.40; dswb2 has 10.4.0.1/32 on br0.20 and 10.5.0.1/32 on br0.30. New client DHCPDISCOVERs on those VLANs are therefore handled locally by dswa2/dswb2, where no relay is present.
- Peers dswa1 and dswb1 do have a relay running (dhcrelay -i br0.10/20/30/40 … 10.0.0.76) and are listening on UDP 67, but that does not help when the broadcast is processed by the non-relaying switch.

Root cause: After the distribution-switch change, isc-dhcp-relay was never configured or started on dswa2 and dswb2.

Corrective fix: On dswa2 and dswb2, configure /etc/default/isc-dhcp-relay with SERVERS="10.0.0.76" and INTERFACES="br0.10 br0.20 br0.30 br0.40 br0.99", then enable and start the service (systemctl enable --now isc-dhcp-relay). Align the interface list with peers and manage both nodes consistently to restore DHCP for all VLANs. === END ===